2026-06-30 15:48:52,741 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-b5e0b169-be53-4350-b402-6ce63675f9e1', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "[TITLE]\nInsights into plastocyanin–cytochrome b6f complex formation: The role of plastocyanin phosphorylation\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nAbstract\nPlastocyanin (PC) is a copper-containing protein that acts as a mobile electron carrier in plants during photosynthesis. In this work, we investigated the role of PC phosphorylation in photosynthetic electron transfer, focusing on interactions with both cytochrome b6f (Cytb6f) and photosystem I (PSI) in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii. While the binding and electron transfer between PC and PSI are well characterized, the interaction between PC and Cytf remains less clear. Using chemical cross-linking combined with mass-spectrometry, we identified 2 potential binding models for PC and Cytf: “side-on” and “head-on.” To evaluate electron transfer, we developed an in vitro system that allowed oxidized PC, formed via light-driven electron transfer at PSI, to reoxidize Cytf. Our data show that a phosphomimetic variant of PC, where phosphorylated PC S49 residue interacts with PetA-K188, displays faster Cytf oxidation, likely optimizing binding and electron transfer between PC and Cytf. Additionally, PC phosphomimetic variants exhibited slower transfer rates than the wild type, suggesting that phosphorylation also modulates PC's interaction with PSI. This regulation likely optimizes Cytf oxidation and electron transfer under conditions of low PC availability, such as during high light stress. Overall, PC phosphorylation appears to play a role in fine-tuning electron transfer between PSI, Cytb6f, and PC, thereby ensuring efficient photosynthesis in dynamic environmental conditions.\nStructural proteomics uncovers the role of plastocyanin phosphorylation in regulating electron transfer between cytochrome b6f and PSI.\n\n[METHODS]\nMaterials and methods\nPSI complex preparation\nPSI complexes were isolated from C. reinhardtii, cc124 WT cells, according to. The cell wall was disrupted by nebulizing a dense culture twice in H1 buffer, containing 330\u2005mm sucrose, 5\u2005mm MgCl2 solution, pH 7.8 (HEPES 25\u2005mm). Cell extracts were then centrifuged and resuspended in H2 buffer, containing 330\u2005mm sucrose, 10\u2005mm EDTA solution, pH 7.8 (HEPES 5\u2005mm), and pottered, before loaded on a step-sucrose gradient, containing 10\u2005mm EDTA solution, pH 7.8 (HEPES 5\u2005mm), in increasing sucrose concentrations (1.8, 1.3, and 0.5\u2005m). Centrifugation was conducted for 1\u2005h, in swing buckets (SW32 Ti) at 100,000 × g, 4 °C. Thylakoid membranes were then collected from the 1.3 to 0.5\u2005m sucrose interface and resuspended in a sugar-free buffer, containing 2\u2005mm CaCl2. Isolated membranes were then diluted to a chlorophyll concentration of 0.8\u2005mg\u2005Chl\u2005mL−1 and solubilized with 1% (w/v) n-dodecyl β-maltoside (β-DDM) by incubating for 20\u2005min on ice. Photosynthetic complexes were separated using a linear sucrose density gradient (average density of 750\u2005mm sucrose, 0.025% β-DDM). Centrifugation was conducted for 16\u2005h, in swing buckets (SW41 Ti) at 200,000 × g, 4 °C. The gradients were then fractioned, and PSI complexes were concentrated using ultrafiltration columns with a size exclusion of 100,000 MW.\nCytb6f complex preparation\nCytb6f complexes were isolated from C. reinhardtii strains that express a His-tagged PetA back-crossed to cc125 strains. Thylakoids were extracted as mentioned above, omitting the step-sucrose gradient to improve yields. Solubilization was conducted on crude thylakoid extracts (chlorophyll concentration was set to 2 to 3\u2005mg\u2005Chl\u2005mL−1) using 0.5% (w/v) 4-trans-(4-trans-propylcyclohexyl)-cyclohexyl-α-maltoside (tPCCαM) by incubating for 45\u2005min on ice. The solution was then loaded on a NiNTA column and washed with increasing imidazole concentration (3 to 18\u2005mm) in 250\u2005mm KCl, 0.01% tPCCαM, pH 7.8 (HEPES 25\u2005mm), before the complexes were eluted using 300\u2005mm imidazole. Cytb6f complexes were then separated using a linear sucrose density gradient (average density of 650\u2005mm sucrose, 0.01% tPCCαM). Centrifugation was conducted for 16\u2005h, in swing buckets (SW41 Ti) at 200,000 × g, 4°C. The gradients were then fractioned, and Cytb6f complexes were concentrated using ultrafiltration columns with a size exclusion of 100,000 MW.\nPC mutation and isolation\nThe heterologous expression of PC was done as described by). The construction of the WT Pet17b-PETE plasmid was described in. Site-directed mutagenesis for the different PC variants was based on. Briefly, WT Pet17b-PETE was amplified via PCR using NEB Q5 High-Fidelity 2× Master Mix in 25\u2005µL reaction volumes containing 10\u2005ng DNA template and 0.5\u2005µm of specific primer pairs carrying the new codon for each PC variant (S-to-A mutation: GCC; S-to-E: GAA; S-to-D: GAT); the PCR protocol was set according to the manufacturer's instructions (amplification was done for 25 cycles with 100\u2005s elongation time each), and annealing temperatures were calculated according to the online NEB Tm calculator. After amplification, 1\u2005µL of PCR product was incubated at RT for 10\u2005min with NEB 10× KLD Enzyme Mix in a 10\u2005µL reaction volume before being used to transform NEB 5-alpha competent E. coli cells, for plasmid isolation and Sanger sequencing (Eurofins Genomics). Plasmids with the correct sequence were used to transform NEB BL21 (DE3) competent E. coli cells for recombinant protein production. Following O.N expression (LB medium, 1\u2005mm IPTG, 100\u2005µg/mL Amp, 0.1\u2005mm CuSO₄), the cells were harvested and resuspended in lysis buffer (KCl 10\u2005mm, Cu(NO3)2 10\u2005mm, PMSF 1\u2005mm, and tricine 10\u2005mm pH 7.8). The cells were then sonicated for a total of 180\u2005s (Branson 250 Digital Sonifier w/ Probe, Marshall Scientific), and supernatant was separated via centrifugation (20,000 × g, 30\u2005min) and loaded on an anion exchange column (DEAE Sepharose CL-6B, GE HealthCare). The columns were washed with KCl 50\u2005mm and eluted with 400\u2005mm KCl. The proteins were then concentrated and salt concentration diluted back to 10\u2005mm, before loading them on a size exclusion chromatography (Superdex 65 10/300 GL on an ÄKTA pure system). The concentration of PC was determined spectroscopically (at 597\u2005nm with an extinction coefficient of 4.7\u2005mm−1\u2005cm−1).\nFDX isolation\nFDX (plasmid: Pet21b:FDX:TEVc:MBP:His6) and Tobacco Etch Virus protease (plasmid: pMHTDelta238) heterologously expressed in E. coli were a kind gift from Prof. Dr. Iftach Yacoby from Tel-Aviv University. Expression and purification were conducted as described in.\nCross-linking\nCytb6f-PC cross-linking was performed as described in. Isolated PC (100\u2005µm in 40\u2005µL) was preactivated with a 5\u2005mm 1-ethyl-3-[3-dimethylaminopropyl]carbodiimide-hydrochloride (EDC) and 10\u2005mm N-hydroxysulfosuccinimide (NHS) solution, pH 6.5 (MOPS 10\u2005mm) for 20\u2005min at room temperature. Then, 5\u2005mm ferricyanide was added before the cross-linker was removed and the buffer was exchanged to Ho buffer, pH 7.5 (HEPES 30\u2005mm) via a PD G25 desalting column followed by ultrafiltration with a 0.5\u2005mL centricon (regenerated cellulose: 3,000 MWCO). Isolated Cytb6f complexes (2.7\u2005µm in 100\u2005µL) were pre-reduced using 5\u2005mm ascorbate, in Ht buffer containing 0.01% tPCCαM, pH 7.5 (HEPES 30\u2005mm). Ascorbate was then removed using a PD G25 desalting column followed by ultrafiltration with a 0.5\u2005mL centricon (regenerated cellulose: 100,000 MWCO) and set back to 100\u2005µL in Ht. Activated PC was then added, in addition to 60\u2005µL of Hm buffer containing 10\u2005mm MgCl2 and 0.0167% tPCCαM (to adjust final detergent concentration), pH 7.5 (HEPES 30\u2005mm). The mixture was finally cross-linked for 45\u2005min at room temperature, before samples were taken for further analysis.\nSDS-PAGE\nFor SDS-PAGE 30\u2005μL of the cross-linked mixture (approximately 42\u2005pmol of total protein) were sampled; supplemented with loading buffer (glycerol 8%, SDS 1.33%, SERVA Blue G 0.007%, bromophenol blue 0.007%, Tris 35\u2005mm pH 8.0), sodium dithionate 167\u2005mm, and sodium carbonate 167\u2005mm; and incubated at 65 °C for 20\u2005min. Proteins were separated on 13% (w/v) SDS-PAGE, with a stacking gel of 4% (w/v). Gels were either stained with Coomassie Brilliant Blue (R-250) or blotted onto nitrocellulose membranes (Amersham). Autofluorescence detection of heme-containing proteins was done in the absence of antibodies, using a SuperSignal West Pico Plus chemiluminescent substrate (Thermo Scientific) for 10\u2005min. The proteins were also then incubated with Cytf antibodies (Agrisera, anti-Cytf, AS06 119), which resulted in the same signal bands. Images were taken using Fusion-FX7 (Vilber Lourmat). Size markers were overlayed using the system's software following acquisition.\nMS preparation and analysis\nBands from relevant Coomassie-stained gels were cut, destained, and typically digested according to. Cross-linked peptide samples were analyzed using an LC-MS/MS system comprising an Ultimate 3000 nano HPLC (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, Massachusetts, United States) connected via an ESI interface (Nanospray Flex, Thermo Fisher Scientific) to a Q Exactive Plus mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Samples were reconstituted in solvent A1 (0.05% trifluoroacetic acid/2% acetonitrile [AcN]/ultrapure water) and loaded onto a trap column (C18 PepMap 100, 300\u2005µm × 5\u2005mm, 3\u2005µm particle size, 100\u2005Å pore size; Thermo Fisher Scientific) at a flow rate of 10\u2005µL/min for 3\u2005min using solvent A1. Peptides were then eluted in backflush mode from the trap column to the separation column (PepSep C18, 75\u2005µm × 15\u2005cm, 1.9\u2005µm particle size, Bruker) at a flow rate of 250\u2005nL/min. Eluents for peptide separation included 0.1% formic acid in ultrapure water (A2) and 80% AcN/0.1% formic acid in ultrapure water (B). The elution gradient was as follows: 2.5% to 40% B over 50\u2005min, then 40% to 99% B over 5\u2005min, with 99% B maintained for 10\u2005min. MS full scans (m/z 350 to 1450) were recorded at a resolution of 70,000 full width at half maximum (FWHM at 200\u2005m/z). Fragmentation spectra (MS2) were acquired at a resolution of 17,500 (FWHM at 200\u2005m/z) using a data-dependent method, where the 12 most intense ions from each full scan were fragmented via higher-energy c-trap dissociation (HCD) at a normalized collision energy of 28 and an isolation window of 1.5\u2005m/z. The automatic gain control (AGC) targets were set to 3e6 for MS full scans (MS1) and 5e4 for MS2, with an MS2 intensity threshold of 8.3E3. Maximum injection times were 50\u2005ms for MS1 and 60\u2005ms for MS2. Ions with unassigned charge states or those with charge states of 1, 2, or ≥8 were excluded from fragmentation.\nAnalysis of PC mutants\nInstruments and buffers were the same as described above. Trap column loading was performed at 25\u2005µL/min for 1.5\u2005min. Peptide separation was carried out using a PepSep C18 column (75\u2005µm × 50\u2005cm, 1.9\u2005µm particle size, Bruker) operated at a flow rate of 250\u2005nL/min. The elution gradient was programmed as follows: 2.5% to 5% B over 5\u2005min, 5% to 35% B over 105\u2005min, 35% to 99% over 18.5\u2005min, followed by a hold at 99% B for 20\u2005min. MS full scans (m/z 400 to 1,800) and fragmentation spectra (MS2) were acquired at a resolution of 70,000 (FWHM at 200\u2005m/z) and 35,000 (FWHM at 200\u2005m/z), respectively. Peptide fragmentation was conducted using a data-dependent approach, fragmenting the 12 most intense ions from each full scan through HCD at a normalized collision energy of 28 and an isolation window of 1.5\u2005m/z. AGC targets were set at 3e6 for MS full scans (MS1) and 1e5 for MS2, with an intensity threshold of 1E4 for MS2. Maximum injection times were 50\u2005ms for MS1 and 120\u2005ms for MS2. Ions with unassigned charge states, or those with charge states of 1, 2, or ≥8, were excluded from fragmentation.\nCross-link identification\nMS raw files were analyzed using MaxQuant 2.4.14.0 and xiSEARCH 1.8.7 against the polypeptide sequences of C. reinhardtii proteins forming the Cytb6f complex (PETx). When PC mutants were analyzed, corresponding mutated sequences were used in addition. Default search settings for identification of peptides cross-linked using EDC were applied. Minimum paired-peptide length was 6. Carbamidomethylation of cysteine was set as fixed modification. Variable modifications included N-terminal acetylation and methionine oxidation. Cross-linked peptide-spectrum matches were filtered to maintain a false discovery rate of 0.01 (MaxQuant) or 0.05 (xiSEARCH). The MS proteomics data have been deposited to the ProteomeXchange Consortium via the PRIDE partner repository with the dataset identifiers with accession nr- PXD060078. A detailed list of all identifications of cross-linked peptides is also available as Supplementary File S2.\nSequence alignment\nSequence alignments were conducted by taking all of the available sequences from Uniprot.org (https://www.uniprot.org/), using the following keywords: PETE (PC), PsaF, and PetA. The data were filtered for duplications of identical sequences (in cases where the same organism has multiple annotations). Organisms were clustered to families and aligned according to conserved regions, with the highest similarity. Reported sequence is numbered according to the sequences of C. reinhardtii (PC-ID: P18068 starting from D48 = D1 here; PsaF-ID: P12356 starting from D63 = D1 here; and PetA-ID: P23577 starting from Y32 = Y1 here). Moreover, the most frequent amino acid is presented (for a full alignment, see Supplementary File S1), when alignments are based on structural localization of the residues.\nStructural modeling\nDocking of PC and PetA was performed using the ClusPro.2 docking server (https://cluspro.bu.edu). The structure of Cytb6f (PDB: 1Q90) was used as a receptor and the structure PC (PDB: 7ZQE) was used as a ligand. Restrains were determined using provided plug-in (https://cluspro.bu.edu/generate_restraints.html), and optimal dockings were filtered base on core distances, between the Cu ligand of PC and the Fe ligand in the center of Heme f. For a full list of docking attempts and detailed restraints, please see Supplementary File S3.\nFast optical spectroscopy measurements\nIsolated PSI complexes from C. reinhardtii, WT strain cc124, were mixed in the presence of PC (0.3 to 6.0\u2005µm) and 331\u2005nm PSI, solubilized in 7.5\u2005mm KCl, 2.5\u2005mm MgCl2, 10\u2005mm ascorbate, 1\u2005mm methyl viologen, and 0.5\u2005mm DAD, pH 7.0 (MOPS 5\u2005mm), when mentioned, isolated Cytb6f complexes were added (2.1\u2005µm). Absorbance was measured post a laser flash using “Joliot type Spectrophotometer” (JTS-150, Biologics) supplied with a “Smart-Lamp” with a dual measuring light usage (705 to 740\u2005nm) and adequate detector filters (P700). To conduct measurements of Cytf fast kinetics, we used a setup of triple measuring light (where 554\u2005nm indicates on Cytf absorbance with a baseline drawn between 546 and 573\u2005nm) and adequate detector filters (BG-39). Actinic light was supplied by an orange ring (630\u2005nm, 300\u2005µE\u2005m−1\u2005s−1). Each test was composed of 4 technical repetitions that were averaged. Each type of PC was tested using 3 different PSI complexes, from individual isolations. For an elaborated dataset and analysis, see Supplementary File S5.\nAccession numbers\nSequence data from this article can be found in the GenBank/EMBL data libraries. Accession numbers for all sequences can be found in Supplementary File S1. All described models are supplied in Supplementary File S4; PDB exports of described best fits are available as PDB files, in Supplementary File S4a to d.\n\n[RESULTS]\nResults\nThe interaction sphere of PC and Cytb6f\nThroughout the photosynthetic clade of Viridiplantae (green algae and plants), the extensively positive charged N-terminal domain of PsaF was shown to be crucial for an efficient electron transfer between PC and PSI. In addition to PSI, PC can interact with Cytb6f, or more specifically the Heme f, which is located within Cytf. Again, the 2 patches of PC seem to have similar objectives in this interaction. The backbone residues of the soluble region of Cytf are conserved throughout the entire photosynthetic lineage. Here, in resemblance to PsaF, the region is positively charged due to the presence of lysines (Fig. 1E). For comparison's sake, we aligned the same orders of plants and algae as before (Fig. 1F, numbering based on the sequence of C. reinhardtii, ID: P23577 starting from Y32 = Y1 here). We observed 2 lysines, in Positions 58 and 65, to be conserved throughout the entire domain (K66 is conserved as well, but it is structurally buried and not facing the interaction plane). In contrast, K121 was featured in almost all available green algae sequences (and some members of plants such as Poales and Funariales) but was replaced by glutamine in plants. The southern loop (between Residues 184 and 192) is, without a doubt, the most variable one. Not only do most of the residues feature no conservation between orders of algae, but the sequence is almost incomparable with other groups of photosynthesizers. Moreover, the length of the loop is largely diverse, with some Cyanobacteriota having the longest, uncharged loops. Despite the divergence in sequences, both green algae and plant exhibit 2 conserved lysines, which are always present on the tip of that loop (although on average, the algal loop is longer in 2 amino acids), at Positions 188 and 189 in green algae, and at Positions 186 and 189 in plants (Fig. 1F; original numbering is 185 and 187 as in). Unlike its interactions with PSI, where the copper must be located in a specific position for the electron to pass through the tyrosine gateway, Heme f is located near the surface of the luminal face of Cytf. Studies have shown that there are several possible orientations in which PC can form a stable interaction, while placing the copper ligand in the proximity of Heme f. In a cyanobacterial mimicking type complex, termed “head-on,” the hydrophobic patch of PC interacts directly with the region of the Heme f. The negative patches have little importance in this case, and it can be explained by repulsion between the negative southern loop and the negativity of the acidic residues of PC. The other, more stable complex termed “side-on,” is more well defined and seems to be dominant in plants and algae (also some Cyanobacteriota). In this conformation, salt bridge formation has a crucial impact on the interactions, similarly to the function of the PsaF loop, where the nonconserved loop of PC (between Residues 58 and 61) interacts with K58 and K65 (also R208, however, it is not present in most plants, but replaced with alanine; Supplementary File S1). The conserved loop of PC (between Residues 42 and 46) interacts with the southern loop of Cytf (between Residues 184 and 192). One more interesting change that was observed specifically in C. reinhardtii is a lysine residue located at Position 164. This residue is situated on the other side of the negatively charged region and, so far, has not been reported in the literature (Fig. 1E).\nCross-linking analysis grants insights on Cytb6f and PC interactions\nSince we aimed to test the complex formation between Cytb6f and PC, we worked in an isolated system from C. reinhardtii. Cytb6f complexes were isolated using mutants where Cytb6f contains a 6xHis-tag at the N’ terminus of petA, and PC was recombinantly expressed in Escherichia coli. The complexes and proteins were then either reduced, using 5\u2005mM sodium ascorbate, or oxidized, using 5\u2005mm ferricyanide. We preactivated PC proteins using the cross-linker 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)carbodiimide-hydrochloride (EDC) and N-hydroxysulfosuccinimide (NHS) for chemical protein–protein cross-linking, as previously described. Excess chemicals were then diluted using size exclusion chromatography and additionally washed out via ultrafiltration and centrifugation. Next, the activated PC* was added to the isolated Cytb6f mixture. To test whether the cross-linking was effective, we fractionated samples from the mixture via SDS-PAGE (Fig. 2A; for full images, please see Supplementary Fig. S1) and performed both blotting with specific Cytf antibody (Fig. 2A, right) and Coomassie staining (Fig. 2A, left). The results clearly show that the cross-linking was efficient, as the band of the noncross-linked PetA subunit (NHS−), which is estimated to be at a size of ∼37\u2005kDa, disappears in the presence of the cross-linker (NHS+) and shifts to ∼50\u2005kDa (as PC is estimated to be at a size of ∼10\u2005kDa). Samples were also digested using trypsin and subjected to a MS analysis (for an elaborated description, see Materials and methods). To increase confidence in our data interpretation, we analyzed 4 technical replicates from 3 independent complex isolations using 2 complementary algorithms: MaxQuant 2.4.14.0 and xiSEARCH (see Supplementary File S2 and Materials and methods for details). Cross-linking events identified by both algorithms were compiled and further examined (Supplementary Fig. S2). Several cross-links could be mapped to specific residues (Fig. 2B). However, in cases where peptide loops contained 2 proximal lysines or carboxylic groups (e.g. PetA: 59-QVLANGKK; PC: 59-DDYLNAPGETYSVK), the exact linkage site could not be confidently determined and is therefore reported as a peptide–peptide cross-link. This peptide analysis showed several cross-linking events (Fig. 2B), mostly of the peptide PC:59-DDYLNAPGETYSVK, with both PetA:56-QVKQVLANGK and PetA:59-QVLANGKK, associated to the cross-linking events of PC:D59/D60 with PetA:K58/K65 (PetA:K66 is detected as well, but it is structurally buried and not facing the interaction plane). In addition, many cross-linking events were also observed with PC:D59/D60 and the peptide PetA:111-NILVVGPVPGKK, which indicates cross-links to PetA:K121, and with the peptide PetA179-IVAITALSEKK, indicating on cross-links to PetA:K188/K189. Surprisingly, the results showed a high abundance of cross-linking events with PetA:157-GQVYPDGKK, suggesting an interaction between PC:D59/D60 from PC and K164 of PetA. In addition, PetA:K207 was shown to cross-link to PC:D59/D60 as well, under these conditions. Moreover, we have identified several intramolecular cross-linked peptides, such as PetA:K121-E125 and PetA:K165-D162 (see Supplementary File S2), which could have occurred due to the structural proximity of these residues and probably resulted from residual EDC/NHS. In addition, PetD:K6 and K15 of Cytb₆f were also cross-linked to PC:D59/60 (Supplementary Fig. S2 and File S2). Such a cross-link to PC is not surprising, as PetD:K6 and K15 are accessible on the stromal side. Similarly, EDC/NHC-dependent cross-linking between PsaF and ferredoxin (FDX) has been described using isolated PSI particles, although the native binding and electron transfer partner is the stromal localized PsaC. Yet, the cross-links between PetD:K6 and K15 and PC:D59/60 are only a minor fraction of the PetA-PC cross-links.\nInterestingly, cross-links with the conserved loop of PC (containing Residues D43, E44, and D45) were not detected and so was the peptide containing PC:D54. Indeed, the peptide on which these residues are located has no lysine (which is required for trypsin digestion), resulting in quite a long peptide segment (PC:24-SGETVNFVNNAGFPHNIVFDEDAIPSGVNADAISR). In order to both diminish the size of the peptide and separate the segments containing D43–E44–D45 and D54, we introduced a single point mutation at Residue S49 (which is facing outward and therefore decreased the chances of misfolding), altering it to a lysine (S49K). This strategy was proven fruitful (Fig. 2C), as cross-linking between this version of PC and Cytb6f resulted in many cross-linking events between D54 (PC:50-GVNADAISR) and all other relevant peptides (except for PetA:K121). In addition, we were also able to detect cross-linked peptides between PC:D45/D54 and PetA:K189/K189, as was expected.\nStructural modeling results in shifted interaction conformation\nAccording to the NMR and cryo-EM structural data, in order to establish a stable complex of Cytb6f and PC, the 2 regions of the acidic patch need to be located in the proximity of the lysines situated on PetA. In plants (Fig. 3A; PDB: 2PCF), the PC loop between Residues 58 and 61 is in a tight interaction with PetA Northern loop (distances are specified in Supplementary File S3; structural alignments are shown in Supplementary File S4). The conserved PC loop (between Residues 42 and 46) interacts with the Southern PetA loop (Residues K186 and K189). In addition to that, PC:D52 (in plants) interacts with both PetA:K189 and R208, which as mentioned, can only be found in some plant's lineages (and interacts with D43 as well). This complex formation led to a core/core distance (between the copper atom and the iron center of Heme f) of 11.9\u2005Å. Alignments of algal-type PC and PetA, based on the location of the copper and backbone (Fig. 3B; PDB: 1Q90, 7ZQE), result in several issues. For one, the shortened nonconserved loop of PC (Residues 58 to 61) cannot access the positive region and form bonds with PetA:K58, even if PC:D59 is in the proximity of PetA:K65. Moreover, R208 is replaced by alanine, resulting in diminished interactions for PC:D54 (which resembles in a way the position of D52 in plants). Bonds between the Southern loop of PetA and PC are quite similar; however, a simple alignment results in a clash between PetA:K188 and PC:E44. In order to tackle these issues, we attempted to improve the model by using a modeling server, ClusPro.2. This server ensures an energetically stable alignment of the 2 given proteins, based on bond formations between them (in all forms). To specify optimal complex formations, we restricted some of the interactions to a hypothetical distance between relevant residues (Supplementary File S3). Since we based our model on plant interactions, we began by restricting interactions between PetA:K58,K65 and PC:D59,D60. To mimic the natural salt bridge formation of this complex, we determined the restricted distances to be between 1 and 5\u2005Å, with 50% chance of required bond formations (see Supplementary File S4, nms_01). This however resulted in a distortion of the 2 acidic residues to be locked on PetA:K65, as their reach was not good enough to form bonds with PetA:K58. Unsurprisingly, the Southern loop of PetA was prominent to the complex formation, as PetA:K189 interacts with PC:D54 and PetA:K188 interacts with PC:E44 (see Supplementary File S4, nms_01). However, the distortion led to an increased distance between the cores to 13.4\u2005Å. Indeed, even in case that the orientation of the Cu center along the Heme plane generates no effect on the possible electron transmission, the increased spatial distance will result in it being less effective, which seems unlikely due to the evolutionary needs of the organisms and deems the model less viable. To entangle this issue, we reevaluated the restrictions posed by the software, in accordance with our MS observations (for a full description, see Supplementary File S3; structural alignments are shown in Supplementary File S4, nms_02). As mentioned before, algal-type Cytb6f has a lysine at Position 121, which resulted in many cross-linking events with PC:D59 and D60 (Fig. 2). In addition, we took into consideration the possibility of bond formations between PC:D54 and PetA:K65 and interactions via the Southern loop of PetA (K188 and K189) with the conserved region of PC (D43, E44, and D45). This model (Fig. 3C; see also Supplementary Fig. S3) results in a core distance of 10.9\u2005Å, as PC twists to the other side, mainly due to the bond formations with PetA:K121 (see Supplementary Fig. S3b), positioning PetA:K65 in-between PC:D54 and E44. Notably, this model also places PetA:K188 in the proximity of S49 (see Supplementary Fig. S3c), which was shown to be phospho-regulated, as previously discussed. According to this model, the interaction that is established resembles a “side-on” model and the formed complex is likely to be very stable. However, since we also observed a lysine in Position 164 of PetA (in Chlamydomonas), which is easily cross-linked to PC:D60/D59 as observed in our results (Fig. 2), we opted to decipher another type of interaction, this time restraining the software to take this interaction under consideration (structural alignments are shown in Supplementary File S4, nmh_07). The resulting model showed very interesting features (Fig. 3D; see also Supplementary Fig. S3). The distance between cores was also relatively short, at 10.9\u2005Å, and in general, this model resembles much a “head-on” type complex. The total bond strength of this model is weak, as in addition to PetA:K164-PC:D60 (see Supplementary Fig. S3e) bond, we observed only PetA:K65-PC:D9 and PetA:K121-PC:S10 bonds (see Supplementary Fig. S3f). Forming such bonds could be favorable in the case where S10 holds a phosphate group, as was previously discussed. Notably, all the cross-linking events used for modeling are highly confident and verified by 2 independent search algorithms, as mentioned above.\nKinetics assessments of the role of phospho-regulation on PC\nIn our structural modeling approach, we observed 2 possible models that could result in a stark complex between Cytb6f and PC, which we termed as “side-on type” (Fig. 3C) and “head-on type” (Fig. 3D) complexes. We also observed that each of these complexes relies on different bonds between the acidic patches of PC and lysine residues situated on PetA and that phosphorylation of PC:S10 or S49 residues could have an impact on their formation. In order to test this hypothesis, we used site-directed mutagenesis and expressed the following recombinant PC variants: PC:S10A, PC:S10E, and PC:S49D. These PC variants were compared to wild type (WT) in P700+ re-reduction experiments (Fig. 4). To do that, we mixed isolated PSI complexes from C. reinhardtii with increasing amounts of PC (0.3 to 6.0\u2005µm) and measured changes in absorbance (705 to 740\u2005nm) following a laser single-turnover flash (laser type supplied with a red dye, Sigma), using a “Joliot type Spectrophotometer” (JTS-150, Biologics). Detection resolution was set to have 100 measurements for 5\u2005s postlaser flash, with an initial delay of 700\u2005µs, since the laser flashes tend to “blind” the detectors in this setup. Our data show that with increasing concentrations of PC, the rate of P700+ re-reduction is increasing (Fig. 4A). We then plotted the graphs and fitted them using a double exponential kinetics (OriginPro, ExpDec2). K2 values were calculated according to, and the results were plotted in regard to PC concentration (Fig. 4B). To compare the differences between PC types, we averaged K2 values, between 1.0 and 6.0\u2005µm (in low concentration, there seems to be some additional effect, as K2 is not constant), and plotted the result as box plots (Fig. 4C). The results show that all proteins can reduce PSI. Moreover, S10A shows no effect on PC–PSI electron transfer rates, while S10E and S49D have a slightly decreased rate.\nIn vitro electron flow shows FDX-dependent cytochrome b6f activity\nUnlike PC–Cytb6f complex binding and electron transfer reactions, the kinetics and mode of interaction between PC and PSI are thoroughly researched and very solid. One reason for this difference is the simplicity of measuring PSI activity. PSI oxidation can be triggered by light, as re-reduction via PC can be readily measured. In contrast, Cytb6f is not directly oxidized by light and therefore kinetic studies had to come up with more creative measurement setups, i.e. measured using fast mixing stopped-flow instruments, taking advantage of differences in its absorbance at 554\u2005nm (with 546 and 574\u2005nm usually serve as a reference baseline). In order to tackle this, we took advantage of the properties of PSI as a PC oxidizer. To establish a direct measurement of Cytf oxidation, we mixed 2\u2005µm of isolated Cytb6f complexes and 331\u2005nm PSI solubilized in 7.5\u2005mm KCl, 2.5\u2005mm MgCl2, and 10\u2005mm ascorbate, pH 7.0 (MOPS). In this assay, methyl viologen, which has an absorbance between 500 and 600\u2005nm, was replaced with recombinant FDX, based on recombinant PETF from C. reinhardtii ( ), which was also postulated to have the function of Cytb6f reducer, as illustrated in Fig. 5A. Initially, we tested the effect of FDX on the kinetics of PSI re-reduction (Fig. 5B). The results show that the addition of FDX does not influence the kinetics of P700⁺ re-reduction. Assessments of K2 did not yield significant differences between the values due to the altered protocol (Fig. 5C; FDX+ Cyt− vs. FDX− Cyt−). Moreover, in our experimental setup, we saw a slight decrease on K2 due to the addition of Cytb₆f, in the presence of WT PC (Fig. 5C; FDX+ Cyt− vs. FDX+ Cyt+). We therefore continued to evaluate the effect on the oxidation of Cytf, by measuring its absorbance (Fig. 5D; for full data set, see Supplementary File S5), using JTS (JTS-150, Biologics). We added 1\u2005µm PC to the mixture, which was kept in darkness (gray background), before we illuminated it for 2\u2005s (white background), followed by 15\u2005s of darkness (4 technical and 3 biological repetitions for each test). In the light phase, we observed a stark decrease in Cytf absorbance, which lasted ∼30\u2005ms, followed by a second phase in which it continued to decrease more moderately until a steady state was achieved. As light was turned off, the trace showed an abrupt drop, followed by a steady, exponential increase, back to the original reduced state. We then added increasing concentrations of FDX (0.5 to 5\u2005µm) and resumed the measurements. The results show no significant effect of the FDX concentration on the initial stark oxidation at light onset. In contrast, the rate of the second phase of Cytf oxidation seems to be diminished, meaning a more net-reduced Cytf, possibly by FDX. This could indicate on a circular electron flow, as postulated. However, one should take into consideration the relatively low redox potential of FDX that could possibly reduce the Heme f centers directly. Notably, at the onset of darkness, the drop showed no significant changes, nor did the rate at which Cytf achieved re-reduction. We then added increasing amounts of PC to the mixture (up to 3.0\u2005µm) and observed a mirrored effect on the traces (Fig. 5E). The rate at which Cytf is oxidized increased, in accordance with the apparent concentration of oxidized PC (which itself is oxidized by PSI). The rates of Cytf dark re-reduction were also affected, as the half-time of the reaction increased in accordance with PC concentration, indicating a lasting effect on Cytf oxidation.\nOur next step was to test whether differences in Cytf oxidation as a result of the single PC mutations using PC: S10A, S10E, and S49D could be observed. To do so, we first assayed P700+ re-reduction. Here, we observed a significant decrease in P700+ re-reduction rates for both S10E and S49D, suggesting that in the presence of Cytb6f, re-reduction of P700+ via S10E and S49D is still slowed down, as observed (Fig. 6A, orange). Notably, the re-reduction of P700⁺ was diminished further for these PC variants, compared to the decrease of the K2 values in the absence of Cytb₆f (Fig. 6A, blue; see also Fig. 4C). These observations hint on a stronger interaction between these PC variants to Cytf, diminishing the apparent PC concentration that could reduce PSI. We then examined the traces of the Cytf oxidation assay (Fig. 6B). As before, we fitted the curves to a double exponential regression (OriginPro, ExpDec2; for further data, see Supplementary File S5, sheet: Fits CytF-light). We observed no significant differences in the total amplitude of these traces between PC variants (nor the values of the amplitude constants, A1 and A2 of the double exponential fit we conducted). We therefore focused our views on the trends of the curves, rather than their amplitudes. To do so, we extrapolated the curves in relation to their steady-state oxidation values (following around 2\u2005s of light exposure). As no differences were observed in these trends for the increasing PC concentrations (the amplitudes are different, but the rates and trends remain the same per variant), we averaged the relative traces for each variant (between 1 and 3\u2005µm of PC, with 5\u2005µm FDX) and presented the results in Fig. 6B. The results clearly show a more moderate initial fast drop at the onset of illumination for both S10A and S10E. However, they did not result in any differences in-between these 2 variants (Fig. 6B). Moreover, the second phase seems more prominent in both of these PC variants, compared to the WT, but resulted in no significant differences (Fig. 6C). Finally, the dark re-reduction of Cytf was also indistinguishable in rate for these 2 variants (Fig. 6D). In contrast, the addition of PC:S49D showed completely different phenotype, as it lacked the initial drop and phase separation at the onset of illumination (Fig. 6B). Instead, the signal decreased rapidly without showing any breaks until it reached steady state with an average t½ of ∼120\u2005ms (compared to ∼830\u2005ms of the WT, see Supplementary File S5, sheet: Fits CytF-light). In addition, the dark re-reduction of Cytf was not slowed down by increasing PC:S49D concentrations, as was observed for WT PC, PC:S10A, and PC:S10E (Fig. 6D; see also Supplementary File S5, sheet: Fits CytF-dark).\n"}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
